Day5
# Disable SELinux as of now. If SELinux is not available then install it.
  1. First login into remote server using below command
      ssh username@host OR
      ssh username@ip_address
  2. Provide required password once prompted after above command.
  3. Check if SELinux is available using either or below commands
      sestatus OR
      getenforce
  4. If both of above command returns "No such command or file" then go to step next step else go to step 6
  5. Install SELinux policies using below command (It is assumed that we are working with RedHat or CentOS based server here)
      sudo yum install selinux-policy-targeted -y
  6. Above command will install SELinux policy on the sever. Now, to disable SELinux policy edit config file under /etc/selinux/config using any editor. Here, we are using vi editor
      sudo vi /etc/selinux/config
  7. Look for SELINUX property and provide value to it as disabled like mentioned below
      SELINUX=disabled
  8. Save the file and reboot server to take enforce this policy on the server.
  9. More on SELinux is available on https://www.redhat.com/en/topics/linux/what-is-selinux
